    "created_at": "2026-04-19T10:50:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-19T10:51:27Z",
    "body": "> `<input type=email value=\"foo@xn--japanese-xy4qg6h355l.com\">`\n\nSame Punycode transformations are used for Cyrillic URLs, why does input type=url not handle them then? Yes, figuring out caret position might be difficult in that case, but in my opinion, this does not warrant throwing a runtime error when this is 1% of cases at most, or for the magical possibility that someday some UI widget would work differently when it's been well over a decade and this never materialized. We are constantly getting issues filed on UI libraries, input masking libraries etc. when some things require us to handle caret position and people just throw in type=\"email\" for proper screen keyboard/a11y/semantics whatever. It is really annoying and very much uncalled for. If we get caret position wrong due to Japanese email that's on us, why won't you let us webdevs do what we want/need to do?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
